{"entries":{"chapter-42-study-frailty|Chapter 42 Study: Frailty":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Frailty (consensus definition): medical syndrome, multiple causes, ↓ strength/endurance and ↓ physiologic function → ↑ vulnerability to dependency/death; should not overlap with disability, and not exhaustively defined by sarcopenia or multimorbidity; screening endorsed for all individuals 70 and older (p617)":["u3"],"copy|Physical Frailty Phenotype (Fried/Cardiovascular Health Study) — 5 criteria: unintentional weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slowness, low activity (p619) ≥3 of 5 criteria = frail; 2 of 5 = prefrail; 1 alone = risk factor only, not frailty itself (p619) — note Table 42-3 instead scores intermediate/prefrail as 1–2 criteria (p621) Weight loss: >10 lb lost unintentionally in the prior year (p620) Weakness: grip strength lowest 20% (stratified by gender/BMI) (p620) Slowness: 15-ft walk time, slowest 20% (by gender/height) (p620) Low activity: lowest 20% kcal/wk; men cutoff <383 kcal/wk (p620) Drawbacks: no cognitive dimension; weight-loss criterion can miss sarcopenic-obesity frailty; thresholds derived from CHS cohort (p620)":["u4"],"copy|Deficit Accumulation / Frailty Index (FI) (Rockwood): unweighted ratio of deficits present ÷ total considered, built from a series of 70 measurements (\"deficits\") (p620, p623); rises nonlinearly with age (Gompertz-like), higher in women, ceiling FI ~0.7 (p623)":["u5"],"copy|Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS): the original seven-category version used clinical judgment and predicted institutionalization and death similarly to the FI (p623). The current CFS has nine levels.":["u6"],"copy|Edmonton Frail Scale (EFS): 9 domains — cognition, general health, function, social support, meds, nutrition, mood, continence, performance (p623)":["u7"],"copy|Cognitive frailty: physical frailty + mild cognitive impairment (CDR 0.5), with AD/dementia excluded; it has been suggested that physical frailty should precede the cognitive impairment, to separate physically driven cognitive decline (p627)":["u8"],"copy|Table 42-3 (p621) catalogs further named frailty instruments beyond FPP/FI/CFS/EFS — Gill Frailty Measure, Brief Frailty Instrument, Vulnerable Elders Survey (VES-13), FRAIL Scale (p621–622) Table 42-3 also spells out the original CFS’s seven categories (p621): 1 = very fit; 2 = well (no active disease); 3 = well, with treated comorbid disease; 4 = apparently vulnerable (not frankly dependent, \"slowed up\"); 5 = mildly frail (IADL help only); 6 = moderately frail (help with IADLs + ADLs); 7 = severely frail (ADL-dependent) or terminally ill. The current nine-point CFS scores terminal illness as 9. VES-13: frail if score ≥3 (range 0–10); FRAIL Scale (fatigue, resistance, ambulation, ≥5 of 11 specified illnesses, weight loss ≥5% over the preceding year): 0 = no frailty, 1–2 = intermediate, ≥3 = frailty (p622; validated FRAIL questionnaire, Morley et al., PMCID PMC4515112).":["u9"],"copy|Community-dwelling age ≥65: average prevalence 11% (reported range 4.0–59% across definitions/settings) (p626)":["u10"],"copy|Broader definitions > Fried tool: 14% vs 10% (p626)":["u11"],"copy|Rises with age: 16% at 80–84 → 26% at ≥85 (p626)":["u12"],"copy|Women > men: Fried 9.6% vs 5.2%; FI 39% vs 37% (p626)":["u13"],"copy|Hospitalized inpatients: 27–80%; institutionalized: 30–70% (p626)":["u14"],"copy|Exhaustion is typically the first CHS criterion to appear (p626)":["u15"],"copy|Nearly 60% of adults >70 transition between frailty states at least once over 4.5 years; progression to a worse state is more common than recovery, and frail→nonfrail reversal is rare (p626)":["u16"],"copy|Concentric layers: outer = clinical presentation (multimorbidity, physical/cognitive impairment); middle = biomarker layer (inflammation, hormones, body composition, energetics); inner = core biological hallmarks of aging (p617)":["u17"],"copy|\"Inflammaging\": chronic elevation of IL-6 and TNF-α, present even in disease-free older adults (p624); accumulating senescent cells spilling SASP molecules may account, at least in part, for the high circulating inflammatory markers (p625)":["u18"],"copy|Multi-hormonal decline: DHEAS, testosterone, estrogens, GH/IGF-1, vitamin D ↓, with relative preservation of cortisol/thyroid hormones (p625)":["u19"],"copy|Muscle energy demand rises as much as 100-fold from rest to contraction — mitochondrial decline + reduced satellite-cell repair capacity impair this reserve (p625)":["u20"],"copy|RMR = 60–70% of total daily energy expenditure; initially rises (compensation), later falls as frailty develops — hypothesized that this RMR shift marks failure of resilience and accelerated, irreversible deterioration (p626)":["u21"],"copy|Surgery: frailty predicts postop pneumonia, delirium, UTI, longer stay, nursing-home discharge, and higher mortality; a multidimensional frailty score was more useful than conventional methods for predicting surgical outcomes (p627)":["u22"],"copy|CKD: CHS participants with CKD had 2-fold risk of frailty + disability; frail patients start dialysis earlier (higher eGFR) with no proven survival/functional benefit (p628)":["u23"],"copy|CVD: frailty ~3× more prevalent with CVD; CAD in women → de novo frailty 12% vs 5% over 6 yr; frail elders develop CAD events at 3.6% vs 2.8%/yr (p628)":["u24"],"copy|HIV: frailty prevalence 5–20% in younger HIV+ patients; risk ↑ with CD4 <350 cells/mm³, further risk factors: CD4 <100 cells/mm³, clinical AIDS, previous opportunistic illnesses (p628)":["u25"],"copy|Transplant: MELD predicts waitlist mortality but predicts posttransplant outcomes only at scores above 35; Fried-criteria frailty tracked quality of life better than MELD severity in end-stage liver disease (p629)":["u26"],"recall|Recall":["u27"],"copy|CHAPTER 42s / HAZZARD":["u28"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 42 — Frailty; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u29"],"copy|Definitions & diagnostic models":["u30"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u31"],"copy|Pathophysiology (\"onion\" model, Fig 42-1)":["u32"],"copy|Frailty in specific disease contexts":["u33"],"copy|1.":["u34"],"copy|What are the 5 components of the Fried physical frailty phenotype?":["u35"],"copy|Unintentional weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slowness, and low activity (p619).":["u36"],"copy|2.":["u37"],"copy|How many of the 5 Fried criteria define frailty, and how many define prefrailty?":["u38"],"copy|≥3 of 5 = frail; 2 of 5 = prefrail; 1 alone is a risk factor, not frailty (p619).":["u39"],"copy|(Table 42-3 scores intermediate/prefrail as 1–2 criteria, p621.)":["u40"],"copy|4.":["u41"],"copy|What distance is walked to test the \"slowness\" criterion, and what defines an abnormal result?":["u42"],"copy|15-ft walk time; abnormal = slowest 20%, stratified by gender and height (p620).":["u43"],"copy|10.":["u44"],"copy|What is the average prevalence of frailty in community-dwelling adults ≥65, and what is the reported range?":["u45"],"copy|Average 11%, reported range 4.0%–59% depending on definition/setting (p626).":["u46"],"copy|11.":["u47"],"copy|How does frailty prevalence differ between ages 80–84 and ≥85?":["u48"],"copy|16% at ages 80–84 vs 26% at ages 85 and older (p626).":["u49"],"copy|14.":["u50"],"copy|What CD4+ T-cell count thresholds are linked to HIV-related frailty risk?":["u51"],"copy|CD4 <350 cells/mm³ (a marker of advanced HIV disease) is independently associated with a frailty-related phenotype; CD4 <100 cells/mm³ is a further risk factor, as are clinical AIDS and previous opportunistic illnesses (p628).":["u52"],"copy|Answer":["u53","u54","u55","u56","u57","u58"],"copy|Table 42-3 (p621) catalogs further named frailty instruments beyond FPP/FI/CFS/EFS — Gill Frailty Measure, Brief Frailty Instrument, Vulnerable Elders Survey (VES-13), FRAIL Scale (p621–622) Table 42-3 also spells out the original CFS’s seven categories (p621): 1 = very fit; 2 = well (no active disease); 3 = well, with treated comorbid disease; 4 = apparently vulnerable (not frankly dependent, \"slowed up\"); 5 = mildly frail (IADL help only); 6 = moderately frail (help with IADLs + ADLs); 7 = severely frail (ADL-dependent) or terminally ill. The current nine-point CFS scores terminal illness as 9. VES-13: frail if score ≥3 (range 0–10). FRAIL Scale: fatigue, resistance, ambulation, more than 5 illnesses, and more than 5% weight loss. Score 0 = no frailty, 1–2 = intermediate, ≥3 = frailty (Table 42-3, p622).Current practice (not the book): the validation study’s Methods scored 5 or more of 11 illnesses and at least 5% weight loss over 12 months. Its appendix uses >5% for weight loss. Morley et al., 2012, Methods and appendix.":["u9"],"copy|CHAPTER 42 / HAZZARD":["u28"],"frailty|Frailty":["u204"],"defining-frailty|Defining frailty":["u205"],"measuring-the-physical-phenotype|Measuring the physical phenotype":["u206"],"counting-fried-criteria-at-the-bedside|Counting Fried criteria at the bedside":["u67"],"deficits-function-and-clinical-scales|Deficits, function and clinical scales":["u207"],"grading-dependence-with-the-clinical-frailty-scale|Grading dependence with the Clinical Frailty Scale":["u68"],"choosing-an-instrument-before-tavi|Choosing an instrument before TAVI":["u69"],"biological-reserve-and-change-over-time|Biological reserve and change over time":["u208"],"energetics-and-the-falling-resting-metabolic-rate|Energetics and the falling resting metabolic rate":["u71"],"pathophysiology-quot-onion-quot-model-fig-42-1|Pathophysiology (\"onion\" model, Fig 42-1)":["u32"],"cognition-chronic-illness-and-prognosis|Cognition, chronic illness and prognosis":["u209"],"when-cognition-is-part-of-frailty|When cognition is part of frailty":["u70"],"prognosis-in-the-patient-with-chronic-disease|Prognosis in the patient with chronic disease":["u72"],"frailty-in-specific-disease-contexts|Frailty in specific disease contexts":["u33"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u210"],"copy|Frailty prevalence was 16% at ages 80–84 and 26% at age 85 or older (p626).":["u211"],"copy|Frailty was more common in women than men with both definitions. Prevalence was 9.6% versus 5.2% with the Fried phenotype and 39% versus 37% with the frailty index (p626).":["u212"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u76"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u213"],"copy|Frailty describes vulnerability to a stressor.":["u214"],"copy|Begin by distinguishing a physical phenotype from accumulated deficits and from dependence in everyday activities.":["u215"],"copy|Start with what changed over the last year.":["u77"],"copy|Ask whether weight loss was unintentional, whether ordinary tasks feel exhausting and how much the patient usually moves.":["u78"],"copy|Observe gait speed over a measured distance and measure handgrip strength.":["u79"],"copy|A person who stays on the couch most of the day may meet the low-activity criterion even with stable chronic diseases.":["u80"],"copy|A cognitive score, ejection fraction or number of admissions cannot substitute for that history (pp.":["u81"],"copy|619–620).":["u216","u229"],"copy|Count domains once.":["u83"],"copy|Three or more of the five define frailty.":["u84"],"copy|Slowness, weak grip, low activity and exhaustion already satisfy four domains.":["u217"],"copy|With the Fried phenotype, very slow walking, exhaustion and a 7-kg loss support three if the loss was unintentional.":["u218"],"copy|Ask about the intention rather than inferring it from the scale reading.":["u87"],"copy|The printed narrative on p619 calls two criteria prefrail and one a risk factor, while Table 42-3 uses one or two as the intermediate category.":["u88"],"copy|Both agree that three define frailty (pp.":["u89"],"copy|619–621).":["u90"],"copy|Physical Frailty Phenotype (Fried/Cardiovascular Health Study) — 5 criteria: unintentional weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slowness, low activity (p619)":["u219"],"copy|≥3 of 5 criteria = frail; 2 of 5 = prefrail; 1 alone = risk factor only, not frailty itself (p619) — note Table 42-3 instead scores intermediate/prefrail as 1–2 criteria (p621)":["u220"],"copy|A fall is an event.":["u91"],"copy|Slowness is a measurement.":["u92"],"copy|For the original Cardiovascular Health Study (CHS) phenotype, time the 15-foot walk and interpret it by sex and height.":["u93"],"copy|A cane, fear of falling, two falls or an unsteady gait may explain reduced activity, but none supplies the timed result.":["u94"],"copy|Likewise, BMI helps interpret handgrip strength.":["u95"],"copy|It cannot replace a grip measurement or a history of weight change (p620, Table 42-2).":["u96"],"copy|Weight loss: >10 lb lost unintentionally in the prior year (p620)":["u221"],"copy|Weakness: grip strength lowest 20% (stratified by gender/BMI) (p620)":["u222"],"copy|Slowness: 15-ft walk time, slowest 20% (by gender/height) (p620)":["u223"],"copy|Low activity: lowest 20% kcal/wk; men cutoff <383 kcal/wk (p620)":["u224"],"copy|The Fried phenotype omits cognition.":["u225"],"copy|Its weight-loss criterion can miss frailty in sarcopenic obesity, and its thresholds came from the Cardiovascular Health Study (p620).":["u226"],"copy|When a vignette omits the measurements, ask which measurements are still missing.":["u227"],"copy|A history alone may leave more than one plausible classification.":["u228"],"copy|The woman’s 2-kg loss is below the original absolute threshold of more than 10 lb, and difficulty cleaning does not measure grip.":["u99"],"copy|The man’s walking difficulty, trouble lifting groceries and exhaustion after transfers suggest three domains that warrant confirmation.":["u100"],"copy|Neither history documents three CHS measurements (pp.":["u101"],"copy|Ask what the patient usually manages alone.":["u103"],"copy|Someone who depends on others for shopping, meals and housework has instrumental limitations.":["u104"],"copy|When the person also needs help bathing or dressing, moderate frailty (CFS 6) fits.":["u105"],"copy|Walking independently with a rollator and living alone do not undo the need for personal assistance.":["u106"],"copy|Use baseline dependence to grade the centenarian (p124, Figure 8-2 and p621, Table 42-3).":["u230"],"copy|A wheelchair user who also needs complete help with personal care can meet grade 7.":["u231"],"copy|The original CFS describes severe frailty at that level as complete dependence for personal care, or terminal illness.":["u109"],"copy|A wheelchair user may transfer, dress and bathe independently.":["u110"],"copy|Ask who performs these tasks before assigning a grade clinically.":["u111"],"copy|Chapter 42 describes the original seven-category instrument.":["u112"],"copy|Chapter 8 reproduces the later nine-point instrument, which has a separate category for terminal illness (pp.":["u113"],"copy|124, 621, 623).":["u114"],"copy|Table 42-3 also spells out the original CFS’s seven categories (p621): 1 = very fit; 2 = well (no active disease); 3 = well, with treated comorbid disease; 4 = apparently vulnerable (not frankly dependent, \"slowed up\"); 5 = mildly frail (IADL help only); 6 = moderately frail (help with IADLs + ADLs); 7 = severely frail (ADL-dependent) or terminally ill.":["u232"],"copy|The current nine-point CFS scores terminal illness as 9.":["u233"],"copy|Use the information the tool requires.":["u115"],"copy|A comprehensive geriatric assessment records diagnoses, signs, symptoms and functional limitations.":["u116"],"copy|An FI constructed from suitable data divides the number of deficits present by the number considered.":["u117"],"copy|Ten deficits among forty assessed gives 0.25.":["u118"],"copy|Different combinations can produce the same fraction.":["u119"],"copy|A shorter FI can be generated from a sufficiently complete clinical record (pp.":["u120"],"copy|620–623).":["u121"],"copy|The original Fried assessment needs timed walking and measured grip.":["u122"],"copy|The FRAIL screen can be administered by asking about fatigue, climbing stairs, walking a block, illnesses and weight loss.":["u123"],"copy|The CFS draws on clinical judgment about baseline function.":["u124"],"copy|The Edmonton scale organizes problems across nine domains so a clinician can see which aspects of vulnerability need attention.":["u125"],"copy|These tools answer different practical questions even when each identifies a patient at greater risk (pp.":["u126"],"copy|621–623).":["u127"],"copy|Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS): the original seven-category version used clinical judgment and predicted institutionalization and death similarly to the FI (p623).":["u234"],"copy|The current CFS has nine levels.":["u235"],"copy|Table 42-3 (p621) catalogs further named frailty instruments beyond FPP/FI/CFS/EFS — Gill Frailty Measure, Brief Frailty Instrument, Vulnerable Elders Survey (VES-13), FRAIL Scale (p621–622)":["u236"],"copy|VES-13: frail if score ≥3 (range 0–10).":["u237"],"copy|FRAIL Scale: fatigue, resistance, ambulation, more than 5 illnesses, and more than 5% weight loss.":["u238"],"copy|Score 0 = no frailty, 1–2 = intermediate, ≥3 = frailty (Table 42-3, p622).":["u239"],"copy|Current practice: FRAIL scale thresholds. the validation study’s Methods scored 5 or more of 11 illnesses and at least 5% weight loss over 12 months.":["u240"],"copy|Its appendix uses >5% for weight loss.":["u241"],"copy|Morley et al., 2012, Methods and appendix.":["u242"],"copy|Table 42-3 compares questionnaire-based tools used before TAVI (p622).":["u243"],"copy|The same table describes the FI on p621, and p623 explains its calculation.":["u129"],"copy|The underlying choice concerns which information can be obtained from an assessment and which measurements must be made directly.":["u130"],"copy|Figure 42-2 links muscle loss to reduced resting metabolic rate and exercise capacity.":["u140"],"copy|Weakness and slower walking limit activity.":["u141"],"copy|Undernutrition and low energy expenditure can perpetuate the cycle.":["u142"],"copy|The direction depends on the stage: the energy cost of compensating and repairing damage may initially raise resting metabolism.":["u144"],"copy|Later, energy production becomes limiting, and resting metabolic rate falls despite ongoing stress (pp.":["u145"],"copy|617–619, 625–626).":["u146"],"copy|The clinical features form the outer layer of the model.":["u147"],"copy|The next layer includes candidate biomarkers of altered signaling, body composition, energy balance and neural function.":["u148"],"copy|The inner layer comprises proposed cellular mechanisms of aging.":["u149"],"copy|Chronic mild inflammation can accompany reserve loss even without overt disease.":["u150"],"copy|A clinician can use physical function to estimate risk without first identifying which cellular process caused it (pp.":["u151"],"copy|617–618, 623–625).":["u152"],"copy|Estimated frailty prevalence with broader definitions versus the Fried phenotype: 14% vs 10% (p626)":["u244"],"copy|These estimates come from particular populations and instruments, so an estimate for a ward should not be applied directly to an independent person in the community.":["u171"],"copy|Frailty is more prevalent at both low and very high BMI.":["u172"],"copy|Muscle weakness and slow gait may be present despite stable weight in sarcopenic obesity.":["u173"],"copy|A shift from robust to intermediate function is clinically meaningful because state transitions can precede dependence (pp.":["u174"],"copy|620, 626–627).":["u245","u246"],"copy|Reduced muscle quality helps explain why strength can fall faster than lean mass.":["u176"],"copy|Motor neuron loss, imperfect reinnervation, fat infiltration, impaired mitochondria and diminished repair capacity can all contribute.":["u177"],"copy|Peripheral motor units become fewer and larger, and sensory conduction and reflexes slow.":["u178"],"copy|Central changes can also compromise executive function, processing speed and multitasking.":["u179"],"copy|These changes affect the ability to perform daily activities even when an individual organ test appears reassuring (pp.":["u180"],"copy|625–626).":["u181"],"copy|In the proposed geroscience framework, frailty, multimorbidity and geriatric syndromes can share aging-related mechanisms.":["u182"],"copy|Higher IL-6 over time was associated with faster accumulation of chronic diseases.":["u183"],"copy|In people who already have obesity, unintentional loss of weight can accompany worsening health.":["u184"],"copy|Researchers are studying whether molecular profiles and measured function can identify groups who respond differently to treatments.":["u185"],"copy|No biomarker panel presented in this chapter replaces clinical assessment (pp.":["u186"],"copy|629–630).":["u187"],"copy|An older adult can meet the physical phenotype with normal cognition.":["u131"],"copy|For cognitive frailty, establish physical frailty and mild cognitive impairment, operationalized here as CDR 0.5, and exclude Alzheimer disease and other dementias.":["u132"],"copy|A screening score alone cannot settle that differential.":["u133"],"copy|The suggestion that physical frailty precede cognitive impairment aims to distinguish physically driven cognitive decline.":["u134"],"copy|It is presented separately from the three operational criteria (p627).":["u135"],"copy|Declining mobility and cognition together identify a group at particularly high risk.":["u136"],"copy|Frail people with a comparable burden of brain pathology have a higher risk of dementia, although the mechanism remains uncertain.":["u137"],"copy|Evaluate new confusion, depression and established dementia on their own merits (pp.":["u138"],"copy|Functional assessment adds information that a diagnosis list misses.":["u153"],"copy|A patient who has recovered from pneumonia on paper may still need help transferring because reserve across systems was limited before admission.":["u154"],"copy|Reduced reserve increases susceptibility to disability, hospital care and death even without a new acute illness.":["u155"],"copy|The consensus described in the chapter supported screening from age 70.":["u156"],"copy|The authors emphasize the limited intervention evidence available at the time and distinguish observational associations with activity or diet from proof that an established syndrome can be reversed (pp.":["u157"],"copy|616–619, 626).":["u158"],"copy|Before surgery, consider postoperative pneumonia, delirium, longer stay and the possibility of discharge to institutional care.":["u159"],"copy|Before dialysis, explain that frailty predicts hospitalization and mortality among new dialysis patients.":["u160"],"copy|Frail patients started at a higher estimated GFR on average.":["u161"],"copy|The observations do not show that an earlier start improves function or survival.":["u162"],"copy|CKD and other illnesses can contribute to frailty, while frailty can also develop independently (pp.":["u163"],"copy|627–628).":["u164"],"copy|Before a cardiac procedure, consider reserve when discussing rehabilitation and the likely care needs after discharge.":["u165"],"copy|In diabetes, CHS participants with frailty had higher glucose and insulin both at baseline and with oral glucose loading.":["u166"],"copy|The direction of causality remains uncertain.":["u167"],"copy|In cancer, assess function, cognition, social support and reserve to estimate tolerance of burdensome treatment.":["u168"],"copy|In transplantation, add the patient’s goals for independence and quality of life to organ-specific estimates of risk (pp.":["u169"],"copy|627–629).":["u170"],"copy|במחקר של מאוחדת נבנה מדד שבריריות אלקטרוני לבני 65 ומעלה.":["u188"],"copy|המדד מרכז ליקויים רפואיים, תפקודיים ופסיכו־סוציאליים שתועדו בתיק.":["u189"],"copy|במשך שנה שיעור האשפוז עלה מ־8% בקבוצה האיתנה ל־19%, 34% ו־48% בשלוש הקבוצות הבאות.":["u190"],"copy|אלה נתוני התיקוף של מדד מאוחדת.":["u191"],"copy|הם ממחישים מה אפשר ללמוד מתיק רפואי עוד לפני שמודדים מהירות הליכה או כוח אחיזה במרפאה.":["u192"],"copy|הרשקוביץ סיקורון ואחרים, הכנס השנתי ה־15 למדיניות הבריאות, 2023, עמ׳ 58, שיטות וממצאים":["u193"],"copy|ברוקדייל עקב אחר מבוטחים בני 65 ומעלה שגרו בקהילה.":["u194"],"copy|החוקרים השתמשו בשאלון הטלפוני VES ובחנו שינויים לאורך שש שנים.":["u195"],"copy|מי שנעשו שבריריים או נשארו שבריריים נטו יותר להתאשפז ולקבל טיפול בית ממי שנשארו ללא שבריריות.":["u196"],"copy|בסקר הראשון השתתפו 608 איש ובסקר החוזר אותרו 281, כך שיש לפרש את השינוי על רקע התמותה והנשירה מהמעקב.":["u197"],"copy|בנטור, שטרנברג ושולדינר, ״שבריריות בקרב זקנים״, 2015, תקציר המחקר וסעיף ״מממצאי המחקר״":["u198"],"copy|כאשר אדם מתקשה להתמיד בפעילות בגלל חולשה או עייפות, משרד הבריאות מציע לבחור פעילות נגישה ומהנה שמתאימה ליכולת ולתפקוד.":["u199"],"copy|אפשר להתחיל בפרקי פעילות קצרים בבית ולהעלות את משך הפעילות או את עצימותה בהדרגה.":["u200"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״פעילות גופנית שמותאמת אליי״, סעיפים ״טיפים לשילוב פעילות גופנית בשגרה היומית״ ו״להיות בתנועה, גם בבית״":["u201"],"copy|2020 Q96 Fried criteria versus age p619":["u247"],"copy|December 2021 Q52 Weight loss in the phenotype p619":["u248"],"copy|June 2022 Q23 Low activity in Fried scoring p619":["u249"],"copy|May 2024 Q62 Identifying the Fried components p619":["u250"],"copy|June 2026 Q45 Phenotype with stable weight p619":["u251"],"copy|June 2023 Q100 Falls versus Fried criteria p620":["u252"],"copy|September 2024 Q3 Counting qualifying domains p620":["u253"],"copy|June 2025 Q93 Classifying the Fried phenotype p620":["u254"],"copy|June 2025 Q49 Measuring grip strength p620":["u255"],"copy|September 2024 Q21 Dependence on the Clinical Frailty Scale p621":["u256"],"copy|June 2026 Q38 Grading ADL and IADL dependence p621":["u257"],"copy|June 2025 Q23 Instrument choice before TAVI p622":["u258"],"copy|June 2025 Q76 Resting metabolic rate p617":["u259"],"copy|June 2026 Q72 Prognosis beyond chronic disease p616":["u260"],"copy|2020 Q20 Postoperative pneumonia risk p627":["u261"],"copy|June 2022 Q12 Frailty at dialysis initiation p627":["u262"],"copy|May 2024 Q54 Cognitive frailty and MCI p627":["u263"]},"maxId":263}
